 not a keyword, a `:gensym`  |\n1062:index, or a projection while stepping. Projection and query are load-time\n1080:;; :global rows are exactly the free names; :variable rows carry a name too, but a bound one contributes no obligation (§7.7.2)\n1176:| `:variable`,  | `:closure`, `:push`,   | a call's effects   |                                                                                                          |\n1192:A name extracted from code (a `:global` row) is an **obligation**. This design preserves named variables across the Semantic Tuple boundary, but `:variable` and `:global` resolve through different paths: the evaluator looks up a `:variable` name in the lexical environment (the closure's captured `sym → value` map, §7.7.3), while a `:global` name resolves through `resolve-var`'s precedence, store → primitives → modules (`engine.cljc:46-58`).\n1194:**Variables never fall through to globals.** An under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an over-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. An unbound variable name evaluates to `nil`; it never falls through to the store, primitives, or modules (since `:variable` and `:global` are distinct nodes). Therefore, name obligations are strictly and statically the set of `:global` names, never `:variable` names.\n1206:sentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n1215:environments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n1216:own environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n1218:closure's captured environment, and nothing else. This context\n1443:  batch member index: the successor of `:yin/source-call` qualified by\n1563: :yin/root          i                                ; index of the tree the evaluator runs\n1622:  declaration ordinal `k` is its index in the declaration catalogue, never\n1623:  its index in the harvest catalogue; every occurrence of that group is\n1696:(`query/current` two-arity, `query.cljc:210-212`; `bound-datoms`,\n1727:The walker stays on map ASTs exactly as built, retaining named variable evaluation. What changes is the\n1734:| walker, `ast_walker.cljc`                        | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2).                                                                                                      |\n1739:| linearizer,                                      | reads the row relation through the same `get-attr`-over-an-index shape it has today (`lower-node`'s `(get-attr e    |\n1740:| `linearize.cljc:87-148`,                         | :yin/type)` becomes a lookup by row id); `ast-children` becomes a table lookup of `node`/`nodes` slot positions;    |\n1741:| `:230-242`                                       | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name`, `:global name`, and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away     |\n1744:|                                                  | `:yin/root` and `:eid` removed; `:yin/address` added; `:global` node added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n1749:| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; frame binding for `:closure` matches arguments to `params` by position, binding each to its name (§7.7.2); `:global` opcode resolves store → primitives → modules; decodes the new operands (`:var name`, `:global name`, `:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n1757:|                                                  | a declaration at its tree index and path, then strip the flag                                                       |\n1808:- **`dao.space.index` and published covered indexes**: they index datoms,\n1871:   captured-environment context abstract